The '''Ruf CTR3''' is a [[mid-engined]] [[sports car]] produced by [[Germany|German]] car manufacturer [[Ruf Automobile]]. The CTR3 was unveiled at the [[Bahrain International Circuit]] on the 20th anniversary of the original [[Ruf CTR]] on April 13, 2007 in conjunction with the opening of a new Ruf factory at the circuit.<ref name=bloomberg>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2007-04-17/ruf-stuffbusinessweek-business-news-stock-market-and-financial-advice|title=German sports car manufacturer RUF opens a new factory at the Bahrain and launches its RUF CTR3 supercar|date=2007-04-17|website=Bloomberg|access-date=2018-08-13}}</ref><ref name=GulfWeekly>{{Cite web|url=https://www.gulfweekly.com/Articles/15114//The-dream-machine|title=The dream machine|date=2007-04-18|website=GulfWeekly|access-date=2018-08-13}}</ref>

Continuing the Ruf tradition of enhancing [[Porsche]] automobiles, the CTR3 shares the body style and engine type with the contemporaneous [[Porsche 987#Cayman|Porsche 987 Cayman]] and [[Porsche 997#Turbo|Porsche 997.1 Turbo]]. For the first time, however, it features a Ruf-designed body built on a dedicated platform engineered in conjunction with [[Multimatic]]<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/car-culture/a20746/2008-ruf-ctr3-1/|title=2008 Ruf CTR3|date=2007-05-21|work=Road & Track|access-date=2017-07-24|language=en}}</ref> and is styled to reflect the visual feel of vintage [[24 Hours of Le Mans|Le Mans]] race cars of the 1950s and 1960s.<ref name=GulfWeekly /> Additionally, Ruf adopted the [[Porsche Boxster/Cayman|Porsche Cayman]]'s mid-engine layout for the new car, rather than the traditional rear-engine layout of the previous CTR models (CTR and CTR2).

==Specifications==
===Powertrain===
The Ruf CTR3 is powered by a {{convert|3746|cc|L|1|abbr=on}} [[twin-turbo]]charged [[flat-six engine]] producing a maximum power output of {{convert|691|bhp|PS kW|0|abbr=on}} at 7,600 rpm and {{convert|889|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of [[Torque#Machine torque|torque]] at 4,000 rpm.<ref name="evo">{{Cite web|url=https://www.evo.co.uk/carreviews/evocarreviews/231960/ruf_ctr3.html|title=Ruf CTR3|last=Vivian|first=David|date=2009-01-19|website=evo|access-date=2018-08-13}}</ref> The cylinder block and heads are made of aluminum alloy with a [[Bore (engine)|bore]] and [[Stroke (engine)|stroke]] of {{convert|102|x|76.4|mm|2|abbr=in}}, and the engine utilizes a [[Overhead camshaft#Dual overhead camshaft|dual overhead camshaft]] [[valvetrain]].<ref name="evo" /> The engine has a [[compression ratio]] of 9.2:1 and is controlled by a [[Robert Bosch GmbH|Bosch]] [[Motronic]] [[Engine control unit|ECU]].<ref name="sa787" />
The flat-six engine is paired with [[Kühnle, Kopp & Kausch|KKK]] K24 [[twin-turbo]]chargers and two air-to-air [[Intercooler|intercoolers]].[[File:Goodwood Festival of Speed 2011 - IMG 2043 - Flickr - Adam Woodford.jpg|left|thumb|The H6-TT engine in the CTR3.]]

=== Transmission ===
The CTR3 is equipped with a transversely mounted 6-speed [[sequential manual transmission]]. The transmission is paired with a limited-slip differential.<ref name="sa787"/>

=== Chassis ===
The CTR3 has a [[Vehicle frame|frame]] constructed from aluminum and zinc-dipped steel for the front and passenger sections of the car.<ref name="sa787"/> The rear frame around the engine is a space frame constructed from [[Billet aluminium|billet aluminum]] by Multimatic that Ruf calls 'the Birdcage'.<ref name=evo/> The body panels are made from a [[kevlar]]-[[carbon]] composite, and the car weighs {{convert|1,400|kg|0|abbr=on}} in total.

